---
/**
 * A broken image must not become 650 px of blank page with the alt text spilling out of its frame.
 *
 * Every image frame in this storefront is already a tinted `bg-brand-50` box with a fixed aspect
 * ratio — the empty-photo state renders exactly that box with a lucide glyph in it. But that branch
 * only runs when the `src` is EMPTY. When the URL is present and the FETCH fails, the browser paints
 * its own broken-image glyph plus unstyled alt text, which overflows, clips, and pushes the price and
 * the buy button a thousand pixels down the page. Observed on a PDP whose media 404'd.
 *
 * It is not a theoretical state: merchant-uploaded photos are `/api/uploads/…`, served by the ADMIN
 * app on a different origin (`data/catalog.ts`), so every admin redeploy is a window where the shop's
 * own product photos do not resolve — and the planned MinIO/S3 move adds a second origin that can fail
 * independently. A PDP with no photo does not sell; a PDP with a wrecked layout sells less.
 *
 * Hiding the failed `<img>` reveals the tinted frame that is already behind it, which is the same
 * empty state the no-photo branch renders. Capture phase, because `error` does not bubble.
 */
---

<script is:inline>
  document.addEventListener(
    "error",
    function (e) {
      var el = e.target;
      if (!el || el.tagName !== "IMG" || el.dataset.imgFailed) return;
      el.dataset.imgFailed = "1";
      el.style.display = "none";
      // The alt text has already been announced to assistive tech by the time this runs; leaving it
      // on a display:none element keeps it out of the accessibility tree too, which is the honest
      // result — there is no image to describe.
      el.setAttribute("alt", "");
    },
    true,
  );
</script>
